{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1315637744/10/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8a6d367c\" lines=\"52-67\">\n<description>\nComplete the framed area by expanding the unique upper template that contains the existing seed pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- A teal background contains small colored template masks above a larger rectangular frame.\n- The frame encloses one fill color arranged as a partial block-scaled copy of one template.\n- The frame interior dimensions are an integer square scale of the matching template bbox.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the rectangular frame.\n- Identify the unique upper template whose block-scaled mask covers every existing fill pixel inside the frame.\n- Reset the frame interior to background and paint the whole scaled mask with the fill color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe lower colored pixels are seed cells that reveal which upper template should be completed inside the frame.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e734a0e8\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nCopy the unique colored sprite tile into every tiled cell marked by an internal separator-colored pixel.\n\nInput structure\n- Full same-color separator rows and columns partition the grid into equal rectangular tiles.\n- One tile contains the only non-background, non-separator colored sprite.\n- Target tiles are otherwise background-colored but contain at least one internal pixel of the separator color.\n\nRule\n- Keep the separator lines and the source sprite tile unchanged.\n- Replace each marked target tile with an exact copy of the source tile.\n- Unmarked background-only tiles remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The internal separator-colored pixels are placement markers; the single colored tile is the template to stamp into those marked cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"bae5c565\" lines=\"10-28\">\n<description>\nErase the top palette row and redraw it below as a widening wedge centered on its missing cell.\n\nInput structure\n- The top row is a palette row with exactly one background gap.\n- Below it, the only foreground is one vertical marker bar of a single color.\n- The marker bar is either in the gap column or immediately beside it.\n\nRule\n- Clear the grid to background.\n- Starting at the top of the marker bar, each next row copies one more\n  column of the top-row palette on both sides of the gap.\n- Fill the gap column with the marker color, and keep the vertical marker bar.\n\nKey insight\n- The lone background cell in the top row is the center of the expanding\n  copy; the lower vertical bar provides the center color and the row where\n  the expansion begins.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"bf45cf4b\" lines=\"7-19\">\n<description>\nScale up a monochrome control sprite by stamping a polychrome tile at each filled pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background color.\n- Two foreground objects (8-connected): a monochrome rectangular sprite with bg holes (control), and a polychrome square tile (block).\n\nRule:\n- For each cell in the control sprite's bbox: if filled, stamp a copy of the block tile; if bg, leave a tile-sized bg region.\n- Output grid dimensions are (control_h × tile_h) by (control_w × tile_w).\n\nKey insight: The control sprite acts as a binary mask that gets \"pixel-scaled\" by the tile — each pixel expands to a full tile copy or empty space.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f0100645\" lines=\"51-68\">\n<description>\nSlide each inner colored object horizontally into the same-colored vertical wall until it touches an obstacle.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The only foreground colors are two non-background colors.\n- One foreground color fills the left column and the other fills the right column.\n- Extra 8-connected objects of each wall color appear away from that color's wall.\n\nRule\n- Clear the extra objects from their original positions.\n- For each wall color, process its 8-connected extra objects from nearest to farthest from the matching wall.\n- Slide each object horizontally toward its matching wall as far as possible without overlapping the wall or an already settled object of the same color.\n- Keep the two vertical wall columns fixed.\n\nKey insight\n- The side columns act like color-specific gravity walls: same-colored objects compact sideways against their own wall while preserving each object's shape.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8698868d\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nMatch sprites to frames by connected-hole count, then nest each sprite inside its frame.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Large hollow squares (frames) arranged in a grid; smaller filled squares (sprites) placed elsewhere.\n- Each frame and sprite has a distinct color. Sprites may contain holes (non-sprite-color pixels).\n\nRule:\n- Frames form a regular grid; some may be bg-colored (invisible).\n- Count connected hole regions inside each frame's interior and each sprite.\n- Pair each frame with the sprite sharing the same hole count.\n- Place the matched sprite inside the frame border; recolor sprite holes to the frame's color.\n\nKey insight: The matching key is the number of connected hole components, not hole area or color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f28a3cbb\" lines=\"42-50\">\n<description>\nCompress each colored ray pixel inward until it touches that color's 3x3 anchor block.\nInput structure\n- A uniform background grid with non-background colors.\n- Each foreground color contains one solid 3x3 anchor block; all other pixels of that color lie on straight 8-neighbor rays aimed at that block.\nRule\n- Keep anchor blocks fixed, erase loose foreground pixels, and for each ray pack its pixels into consecutive cells adjacent to the anchor along the same ray.\nKey insight\n- The scattered pixels are displaced pixels on visible row, column, or diagonal sightlines back to their same-colored 3x3 block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2a28add5\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nPaint each pink control pixel as a teal horizontal bar whose reach equals the foreground counts on that row.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color and one or more pink control pixels.\n- Each marker row has exactly one pink pixel; all other non-background pixels\n  in that row are counters.\nRule\n- Start from an all-background output grid.\n- For each pink pixel, count non-background pixels strictly to its left and\n  strictly to its right in the same row.\n- Paint teal from c - left_count through c + right_count on that row.\nKey insight\n- The foreground pixels are not copied; only their counts on each side of\n  each pink marker encode the teal bar length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"342dd610\" lines=\"18-36\">\n<description>\nMove every foreground pixel by the fixed offset indicated by its color.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a unique dominant background color.\n- The foreground is a sparse set of colored pixels.\n- Each foreground color is one of the trained motion colors: orange, red,\n  maroon, or blue.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the canvas to the background color.\n- Translate orange pixels up 2 cells, red pixels left 2 cells, maroon\n  pixels down 2 cells, and blue pixels right 1 cell.\n- Preserve each moved pixel's original color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe foreground color is the motion instruction; position only determines\nwhere that color-specific displacement lands.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ba1aa698\" lines=\"41-55\">\n<description>\nExtrapolate the moving sprite from a horizontal sequence of shared-border frames into the next standalone frame.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is a row of equal-width framed panels sharing their vertical border columns.\n- Each panel has the same fill color and one identical interior sprite at the same local columns.\n- The sprite's top row changes by a constant amount from panel to panel.\n\nRule\n- Build one output panel with the same height, frame color, fill color, and sprite.\n- Continue the sprite's diagonal motion one more panel to the right.\n- Because the output is a standalone panel, advance by the full panel width, not the shared-border spacing.\n\nKey insight\n- The panels are consecutive frames of motion; the output is the next frame after the sequence.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5034a0b5\" lines=\"16-32\">\n<description>\nShift each interior pixel whose color matches a frame side one cell toward that matching side.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a one-cell-thick rectangular frame: the four corners are the\nbackground color, and each side between corners is a solid, distinct side\ncolor. The interior is mostly background with sparse foreground pixels.\n\nRule\nInterior pixels with the top-side color move one row up, bottom-side color\nmove one row down, left-side color move one column left, and right-side color\nmove one column right. A pixel already adjacent to its matching side stays in\nplace; colors that do not match a frame side and the frame itself are unchanged.\n\nKey insight\nThe colored frame sides act as direction labels for matching interior pixels.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2e65ae53\" lines=\"88-103\">\n<description>\nFill every lattice slot from the color assigned to that slot position by the other lattices.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The largest foreground color forms several one-pixel-thick rectangular lattice frames.\n- The frame lines divide each lattice into the same row-by-column set of rectangular slots; some slots already contain one non-frame color.\n\nRule:\n- Infer a color for each logical slot position from the colored slots across all lattice frames.\n- Repaint every slot interior in every lattice with the inferred color for its row/column position.\n- Keep the frame lines and all background outside frames unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Slot sizes vary by frame, but the complete color pattern is indexed by lattice slot position rather than by absolute pixel size or location.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"79cce52d\" lines=\"29-44\">\n<description>\nCrop off the control row and column, then cyclically roll the payload by the two marker offsets.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is square, with a control row on top and a control column on the left.\n- The control row and column share one filler color and one single-pixel marker color.\n- The lower-right square is the payload and contains none of the control colors.\n\nRule\n- Remove the top control row and left control column.\n- Roll the remaining payload down by the marker's zero-based position in the left control column.\n- Roll the payload right by the marker's zero-based position in the top control row.\n\nKey insight\n- The two border markers are cyclic shift amounts for the payload, not colors to preserve.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"c7f57c3e\" lines=\"22-35\">\n<description>\nSwap every sprite to the opposite prototype while preserving scale and position.\n\nInput structure:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
